<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsRoslynComponent>true</IsRoslynComponent>
    <EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>true</EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS1591</WarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- This project *is* the analyzer, so nothing here is meant to be referenced
         directly; the package puts the assembly under analyzers/ instead of lib/. -->
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <!-- An analyzer package carries no lib/ and must declare no dependencies: Roslyn is
         supplied by the host. NU5128 is the "no lib for the declared framework" warning,
         which is the intended shape here rather than a mistake. -->
    <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Debugging">
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <DebugType>portable</DebugType>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Package">
    <PackageId>FluentRoslyn.Templates</PackageId>
    <!-- Versioned in lockstep with FluentRoslyn, permanently, and not merely because
         one repository and one tag make that convenient.

         TemplateLifter emits FluentRoslyn API calls as string literals, and this
         package deliberately declares no dependencies (see SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking
         above), so the coupling to a particular FluentRoslyn API surface is invisible
         to the compiler and inexpressible in NuGet. That makes the matching version
         number the only thing telling a consumer which FluentRoslyn this was verified
         against — which is why a release where this package's source did not change
         still ships a new version rather than standing still. The no-op version is the
         assertion that the pairing was tested.

         Bump both, or the release fails before anything is pushed. -->
    <Version>0.1.0-preview.8</Version>
    <Authors>Cameron Lambert</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Cameron Lambert</Copyright>
    <Description>Compile-checked templates for FluentRoslyn. A meta-generator that runs on your source-generator project: mark a method [Template] and its body is real, compiled, refactorable C# that the meta-generator lifts into the FluentRoslyn calls which reproduce it. Breaking a template is an error in your own build, not a CS8785 warning in your consumer's.</Description>
    <PackageTags>roslyn;source-generator;codegen;code-generation;template;meta-generator;fluent;csharp</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/keybindings/FluentRoslyn</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/keybindings/FluentRoslyn</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <DevelopmentDependency>true</DevelopmentDependency>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.1.0-preview.8

No changes to this package, for the third release running. It is still identical to 0.1.0-preview.5, its only substantive release; it shares a version line with FluentRoslyn, which does have changes here. If you already have preview.5 or later there is nothing to gain by upgrading this package on its own.

0.1.0-preview.7

No changes to this package, for the second release running. It is identical to 0.1.0-preview.5, which was its first and so far only substantive release; it shares a version line with FluentRoslyn, which does have a change in this release.

0.1.0-preview.6

No changes to this package. It is identical to 0.1.0-preview.5 and there is no reason to upgrade for its own sake; it shares a version line with FluentRoslyn, which does have changes in this release.

0.1.0-preview.5 — first release, alongside FluentRoslyn 0.1.0-preview.5.

Mark a method [Template] and its body becomes real, compiled, refactorable C#. The meta-generator reads your generator project's own syntax trees and lifts each template into the FluentRoslyn calls that reproduce it, so breaking a template is an error in your own build rather than a CS8785 warning in your consumer's — a warning that leaves the build green and the generated file silently missing.

Holes are marked in the template and filled at generation time. Diagnostics are reported under the FRT prefix.

The ceiling, stated rather than discovered later: a template is checked against the types your generator project can see. The consumer's types exist only when the generator runs, so anything a hole reaches into stays unchecked at that seam. "Type safe, almost" is the honest description.

Install into the source-generator project itself, not into the consumer.</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="4.9.2" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Release tracking for the FRT diagnostics; RS2008 requires it of analyzer projects. -->
    <AdditionalFiles Include="AnalyzerReleases.Shipped.md" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="AnalyzerReleases.Unshipped.md" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="FluentRoslyn.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\assets\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <!-- The analyzer itself. A generator loads from analyzers/dotnet/cs/. -->
    <None Include="$(OutputPath)\$(AssemblyName).dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
